OpenCV @OPENCV_VERSION@ + MinGW-w64 GCC @GCC_VERSION@
Windows x64 / UCRT / POSIX threads / SEH exceptions
OpenCV configuration: @BUILD_CONFIGURATION@ / libraries: @LIBRARY_LAYOUT@
Debug information: @DEBUG_SYMBOLS@
OpenCV CPU optimization policy: @CPU_OPTIMIZATION_POLICY@

This package contains the OpenCV C++ SDK, matching standalone MinGW-w64
compiler toolchain, CMake, Ninja, required OpenCV runtime DLLs, and a small
source example. It is intended for Windows 10 and later on x86-64 systems.

GET STARTED

For the installer edition, open "Developer Command Prompt" from the Start
menu. For the ZIP edition, extract the complete top-level folder and run:

  cmd /D /K "C:\path to package\activate.cmd"

In an existing Command Prompt, use:

  call "C:\path to package\activate.cmd"

Activation changes only the current command prompt. The installer does not
change the machine or user PATH. Use a fresh developer prompt when switching
between installed toolchains.

Build and run the source example:

  build-example.cmd

The example build is created under your LocalAppData\OpenCV-MinGW\examples
folder. See examples\smoke for the editable C++ and CMake sources.

YOUR OWN CMAKE PROJECT

  find_package(OpenCV CONFIG REQUIRED COMPONENTS core imgproc imgcodecs)
  target_link_libraries(your_program PRIVATE ${OpenCV_LIBS})
  target_compile_features(your_program PRIVATE cxx_std_17)

Run CMake from the developer prompt with the Ninja generator. OpenCV_DIR is
already set to this package's CMake configuration directory. Use the bundled
GCC toolchain consistently when building applications that link these DLLs.
Select this package's configuration explicitly:

  cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=@BUILD_CONFIGURATION@

The included example checks that CMake selects real import libraries and DLLs
for this configuration, and that the loaded OpenCV runtime agrees.

DEBUG EDITION

The Debug edition installs separately from Release and contains modular
OpenCV DLLs ending in d.dll, matching import libraries and embedded DWARF
symbols. Use a Debug consumer configuration and the bundled GDB debugger.
Release and Debug DLLs must not be copied into the same runtime directory.

Debug is built with CV_DISABLE_OPTIMIZATION=ON and an empty CPU_DISPATCH.
OpenCV CPU intrinsics, optional instruction-set dispatch and explicit loop
unrolling are disabled to avoid the Win64 GCC AVX stack-alignment failure
in unoptimized code. The compiler's -g -O0 Debug settings and full DWARF
symbols are retained. Debug is intended for stepping through library code;
use the optimized Release edition for performance measurements.

OpenCV source files are not bundled. For source browsing, check out the exact
OpenCV and contrib commits recorded in build-lock.json. In GDB, map the
original build source directory to your checkout with:

  set substitute-path ORIGINAL_SOURCE_DIRECTORY LOCAL_CHECKOUT

The package verification checks that GDB can resolve an OpenCV function to
its original source filename and line; no source download is required for
that symbol check. Debug symbols are not stripped to reduce download size.

FILES AND DEPLOYMENT

  opencv\                 OpenCV C++ headers, import libraries, DLLs and data
  mingw64\                Complete matching compiler toolchain and its tools
  examples\smoke\         Small C++ example and its CMake project
  build-info.json          Versions, source commits and package metadata
  build-lock.json          Exact upstream release/source download information
  activate.cmd             Configure the current command prompt

OpenCV DLLs and their non-system runtime dependencies are in:
  @OPENCV_BIN_DIR@

When deploying a compiled application to another computer, copy its required
OpenCV and runtime DLLs beside the application's EXE. Keep the supplied license
and notice files with redistributed components.

@PYTHON_INSTRUCTIONS@

Read build-info.json and the OpenCV build information for the features
actually included in this build.

LICENSES

OpenCV and each third-party component keep their own license. OpenCV's
installed license files are retained under opencv\; toolchain license files
are retained under mingw64\. See LICENSES.txt for the component source links.

